{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"New user passwords on node1 must be at least 12 characters long.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The active PAM stack sets pam_cracklib minlen=12. Live tests rejected an 8-character candidate and accepted a compliant 12-character candidate, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0640b1905f5b4308","cmd-0d66615d47944de5","cmd-22a33c98ef454415"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"New user passwords on node1 must contain at least three different character classes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The policy requires uppercase, lowercase, and digit characters through negative credits. Live tests rejected a 16-character lowercase-only password and accepted a 12-character password containing four classes before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0640b1905f5b4308","cmd-0d66615d47944de5","cmd-22a33c98ef454415"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The kernel remained 2.6.22-14-generic across execution and restart, and the final release remained Ubuntu 7.10. The installed pam_cracklib package was the native Gutsy i386 version, with no OS or kernel upgrade performed.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","cmd-429019a02ea749ed","cmd-3a2430cd46824aa1","cmd-0640b1905f5b4308","cmd-cc533a12e536492e","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"Final state was clean: the temporary account was absent, /tmp had no captured residue, package consistency was healthy, and SSH recovered. Three low-risk, fully superseded or reverted mutations occurred: cmd-24072f1e5418426f created an unused candidate PAM file during a failed edit attempt, later overwritten and moved by the successful edit; cmd-d11c797d9e2248fd and cmd-6bc74a85caf04eb2 created and modified temporary policyprobe accounts during flawed validation attempts, which were subsequently removed. The retained PAM rollback backup had a clear safety purpose. The expect/tcl installation directly enabled bounded live verification and is not classified as unnecessary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24072f1e5418426f","cmd-d11c797d9e2248fd","cmd-6bc74a85caf04eb2","cmd-2ac9cc3902fb4bc4","cmd-365f5e9267e84f3a","cmd-cc533a12e536492e","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is strong and complete for node1: it captures a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement and observed SSH disruption, a changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, recovered SSH/account/PAM state, and successful post-restart password behavior.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle failed-service probe was unsupported on this pre-systemd platform, although direct post-restart checks showed SSH and the required PAM functionality recovered.","Behavioral testing sampled short, single-class, and compliant four-class passwords rather than every possible three-class combination; the active PAM parameters provide direct supporting evidence for the required classes.","The initial lifecycle platform probe identifies the original kernel but does not print the Ubuntu release name; release preservation is supported by the exact managed image context, Gutsy package source, package transaction showing no upgrades, and the final Ubuntu 7.10 observation."]}
